  "local_bulletin": {
    "source_name": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD)",
    "text": "Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) — forecast for Kisumu\nValid for 2026-09-14.\nMax 28.0C / Min 20.0C.\nTonight: Partly cloudy\nMorning: Sunny intervals\nAfternoon: Sunny intervals, moderate showers and thunderstorms expected over few places.\nNational outlook mentions this area:\n  - Rainfall expected in some parts of Kericho, Kakamega, Vihiga, Nandi, Kisumu, Vihiga, Baringo, West Pokot, Bomet, Homa",
    "fetched_at_utc": "2026-09-14T03:52:14.332310Z"
  },
  "yesterday_verification_summary": "Across yesterday's target verification dates, short-range Day+0 forecasts performed exceptionally well on precipitation occurrence, with all guidance accurately capturing rain while under-forecasting peak surface wind speeds. Day+3 outlooks similarly hit the rain event across all models, though temperature high forecasts showed mixed skill. At Day+7, ECMWF and Best Match correctly verified precipitation, whereas GFS falsely called for dry conditions.",
  "narrative_markdown": "## Overview\nSlightly cooler and much calmer than yesterday. Dry until evening thunderstorms. Temperatures and winds much the same through Thursday, with rain becoming more likely.\n\n## Today's Forecast\nMaximum temperatures will reach 28.8°C / 84°F today, under partly cloudy skies transitioning to late afternoon convective development. Solar exposure will peak with a Very High UV index of 9.0 around midday. Precipitation is expected during the late afternoon and evening (16:00 - 20:00 EAT), carrying an 85% probability of moderate showers and thunderstorms yielding around 6.5 mm (0.26 in) of rainfall, persisting into the overnight period down to a low of 18.2°C / 65°F. Sustained surface winds will peak near 22 km/h (12 kt). Air quality readings across local ground sensors range from AQI 8 (Good) at Kisumu Airport up to 117 (Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups) at Dunga Beach, which is currently the highest reading in the network. Observed conditions as of 06:50 report rain recorded from 01:00, no thunder, a temperature high so far of 22°C / 72°F, a low of 20°C / 68°F, peak gusts of 11 km/h (6 kt), and cloud cover at 5/8.\n\n## Extended Outlook\nNear-term guidance through Day+3 (Tuesday through Thursday) indicates a wet and warm pattern across the Nyanza Basin. High temperatures will range between 29.4°C / 85°F and 32.4°C / 90°F on Tuesday and Wednesday, before cooling slightly on Thursday to around 29.1°C / 84°F with an 85% probability of widespread rainfall reaching 9.5 to 12.9 mm (0.37 to 0.51 in). From Day+4 through Day+7 (Friday through next Monday), confidence decreases in precipitation timing, though elevated shower potential remains active with Day+7 rain probability standing at 60% and maximum temperatures averaging 25.8°C / 78°F to 30.0°C / 86°F.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\nConvective energy builds sharply this afternoon and evening across the basin, with peak CAPE modeled at 830 J/kg on GFS, 1340 J/kg on ICON, 1770 J/kg on Best Match, 2100 J/kg on ECMWF, and an extreme 5000 J/kg spike on UKMO around 18:00 EAT. Any thunderstorm that develops will present a risk of violent, localized downdraft gusts far exceeding the day's forecast wind speed, regardless of how light ambient winds appear. Solar UV exposure peaks at a Very High 9.0 around midday, requiring sun protection. Air quality at Dunga Beach is at AQI 117 (Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups), so sensitive individuals should limit prolonged outdoor exertion.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\nBoaters on Winam Gulf should prepare for deteriorating conditions late this afternoon and evening. High convective potential across the open water will generate moderate showers and thunderstorms, with precipitation probability reaching 100% after 16:00 EAT. Ambient wind speeds over the Gulf will range from 16 to 22 km/h (9 to 12 kt), but localized thunderstorm downdrafts can trigger sudden gusts up to 40 km/h (22 kt) and choppy waves. Boaters should seek safe harbor before storm onset during late afternoon.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n### Synoptic Overview\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the northeast (1006 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1018 hPa) — a 12 hPa spread, a strong large-scale gradient. The lower pressure to the northeast is filling over the next three days. Pressure is also falling toward the north — a large-scale feature building in that direction, though this sampling cannot say how fast or whether it will reach here. Pressure overhead is near-steady. Across the Nyanza Basin, mean surface pressure sits near 1014.7 hPa with a steady 24-hour trend (+0.6 hPa). Strong low-level moisture combined with daytime heating will drive afternoon convective initiation along lake-breeze boundary convergences.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\nModel selection for today's call leans on Best Match and ECMWF IFS, which demonstrate superior long-run verification performance. Review findings establish Best Match as the strongest Day+0 rain caller (85% accuracy) and GFS as the weakest (62% accuracy). GFS continues to show a systematic daytime warm bias (-1.0°C mean error at Day+0, -2.2°C at Day+3) and wind speed under-forecasting (+20 km/h error), while ICON exhibits a nocturnal cold bias on minimum temperatures (-2.5°C error). The Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) local bulletin calls for a high of 28.0°C / 82°F and low of 20.0°C / 68°F today, with sunny intervals in the morning and moderate showers and thunderstorms over a few places in the afternoon, aligning closely with numerical guidance on rain occurrence. Individual ground air quality reporting stations record AQI 8 (PM2.5 8.0 µg/m³) at Kisumu Airport, AQI 46 (PM2.5 46.0 µg/m³) at Ochieng' Avenue, and AQI 117 (PM2.5 117.0 µg/m³) at Dunga Beach. CAMS model data shows background PM2.5 peaking at 31.8 µg/m³ (AQI 65 Moderate) this evening, confirming that elevated readings at Dunga Beach represent localized coastal activity. The numerical guidance behind this issuance was last fully aligned at 18:00Z (9.8 hours old), which contributes to timing uncertainty regarding exact storm onset.",
